2d plans are great but how can you  actually visualize the building that  you've been designing today i'm going to  show you exactly how to design  elevations in arcade 25. if you have no  idea what an elevation is today i'm  going to show you and explain to you  exactly what and how to make an  elevation in archicad 25. elevations are  basically the depiction of the floor  plan from a front on or side on view so  basically you're looking at this floor  plan that you see here on the screen  around all four perimeters i've gone  ahead and created this very very basic  box building to be able to demonstrate  the general fundamental basics of  elevations so if we start off with our  four elevation markers on the side  you'll see that we can use the ones  available on the arcad 25 template or we  can find the elevation markers here on  the left hand panel side it's  basically the one little black triangle  with the line underneath it if you're  looking for in the document palette  let's start by simply dragging all of  these elevations across to exactly where  they need to be now for the purposes of  this i'm going to drag them anywhere on  this document but you should be aware  that elevations are typically seen from  the boundary itself unless there is  something else that dictates where the  elevation must be seen from so if we  take these four elevation markers space  them around the building itself you'll  see the extent of the elevation is  showcased by these two black hot spots  at the end of each marker so if i click  on one of these hotspots go to my  stretch tool i can drag it down to as  low or as far away as i need from this  building in this instance it doesn't  really matter but if you want to  showcase more or less the building you  absolutely can now these four elevation  markers if we zoom in we'll see e03 e02  e01 and e04 respectively if you look at  them a little bit closer e03 indicates  on the right hand panel what that  elevation is actually called and a 301  indicates what page up the top in our  layout book we have actually placed that  elevation on so basically you know if  you look at that elevation on a printout  you'll find it on page 301 despite what  page you found the original marker on  now let's start by clicking on e01 and  experiencing what this looks like  generically straight out of the arcade  box as you can see we have our markers  on the right and left hand side that  indicate the levels if we press command  or control 7 we can change our story  settings here if we don't want to see  these blue markers on the right hand  side we just simply untick press ok and  they will disappear however for the  purposes of this we'll keep them on just  so we can showcase what they look like  again if i press command 7 and let's say  i change this ground floor plan to 27  press ok you'll see that automatically  adjust and automatically update to 7.  you can see that we've cut through the  ground itself we've showcased three  windows on the side and we've showcased  our very very basic building now  depending on what stage you're  showcasing and documenting an elevation  on the scope can be vast and wide it  could be as simple as what you see on  this picture right here today or it  could be extremely detailed outlining  individual elements so for example we  could use a text tool here change our  settings two and black and type in  vertical monument cladding  steel  by builder if we aren't actually  documenting the specific cladding and  then draw our line tool underneath  pointing to that cladding  and changing an arrow so we'd repeat  that same step for the window frames the  doors and everything between the roof  the barge the gutter everything we need  to showcase and detail there are a  number of different ways you can utilize  this you can use keynotes which is a ci  tools plugin you can use basic text you  can do a whole bunch of different  varieties basic text which means you  manually have to go back and override  every single time is probably the most  common and the most basic in arcade 25  after that you move to a bit more  advanced options and tools if we came  back to our ground floor plan now found  our elevation marker one and pressed  command t  we can see that our cut elements are cut  filled and non-shaded we can also see  that our uncut elements are the surface  colour fill if we didn't want that  elevation to be in full colour full 3d  we could simply go uniform pen colour  fill non-shaded press ok  and then double click on our elevation  again you'll see that automatically  update to a black and white line weight  option now i have made every single one  of these line weights exactly the same  so if we went up to view on screen  options and down to true line weight we  would see the exact line weight that it  would be printed now if i was to open up  this window setting go command t  and overwrite my line weight here from  0.3 to  0.25 not only would you see it as a  different color you would also see it as  a much thicker red line  so once you're in elevations it's very  important that we get our line weights  correct and we get all of our  information that we want to portray  across correct so if i undo that step  that window doesn't need to be read in  any way shape or form it would be a  different line way it would be something  else but for this instance we're just  showing you how to set up elevations  correctly now in an elevation you do not  want to see the cut through of the site  in full texture hatch now there are a  number of ways we can alter this we can  do that in 2d we can change our material  settings but for the purpose of this we  are literally going to go at our line  tool let's change that to a nice green  so we know where our line is change that  all the way up the top go to our fill  tool let's go 100  background and we'll make that white and  we'll simply just cover that entire  space there so if i change that get rid  of the line and make sure our green line  is to the front  we will now see that is our natural  ground level so if i replicate my text  we can go n g l standing for natural  ground level and fgl for future ground  level in most instances we now know that  our house sits approximately 86  millimeters below  the natural ground level which we would  indicate with some sort of dimension  markers we could use our dimension text  there outlining all three elements  clicking ok 86 three meters and we could  simply change that  to all black or whatever color we wanted  so that we knew exactly where our  natural ground level was and where our  building started now if we were to go  back to our ground floor plan we could  then again go command t for the purposes  of this on our elevation marker change  our uncut elements back to a texture  fill non-shaded go okay double click and  once again you'll see a very different  example of what that elevation could  look like now what you'll also see is  the shadows in this elevation that are  quite harsh and quite strong so if we  went back to elevation marker command t  we can scroll down to our sun  positioning here now our sun can be  custom at 45 degrees 45 degrees which  allows every single elevation to  replicate the exact same sun position  and keep it relatively clean and visible  some people might actually prefer to  have it as in 3d window so if we click  as in 3d window click ok and then if we  come up to view 3d view options and 3d  projection settings  then we can actually change our date and  time this is actually quite critical  because most of the time you're going to  be looking at the sun path in the winter  solstice here in australia that's on the  21st of the 6th and we'll do it as 2022  in this instance go back to our number  one elevation we'll see that those sun  paths rapidly change and those shadows  are completely different now you'll also  notice the shadows in this building are  for some reason white so if we go back  to our settings command t scroll down to  the bottom you'll see our shadows they  are 50  opacity let's change that 25 let's  change that to black go okay  let's go back to elevation one and now  you'll see those shadows a little bit  more pleasing and a little bit more  architectural we would have another  building behind it just to the right  backhand side now coming into elevation  marker three you'll see that building  appear in the back right hand side  very hard to differentiate what's  further what's closer if we were to  click on elevation mark three go command  t  and scroll down to our  fade distance elements we can quickly  press ok  change our distance marker to include  just this building and everything passed  to be in a different shade so clicking  on elevation 3 again we will see that  our main building is in focus and our  further building has been shaded out in  a light gray defined by the actual  settings anyway that's all for me today  guys thank you so much for watching i  hope you enjoyed the video if you did  like buttons down there subscribe  buttons down there and like always i'll  see you next monday

